WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.430
Hello, everybody.

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:08.655
My name is Ellis Hughes.

00:00:08.655 --> 00:00:11.030
And as you can tell, probably
from this title slide here,

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:12.480
I work at GSK.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.830
I'm a data science leader in
the statistical data sciences

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:19.370
and innovation hub, same as
Becca, who presented earlier.

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:23.240
And we're responsible for
innovation and developing

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.623
of R packages to try to
improve processes and whatnot.

00:00:25.623 --> 00:00:27.290
So I thought I'd come
here today to talk

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:29.080
about that a little bit.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.390
So I think sorry.

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:33.760
I think we've all
worked on a process

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:35.980
before that promised the
world that it was supposed

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:39.880
to solve all our problems, make
our lives just substantially

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:40.880
easier.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:41.930
And it didn't do that.

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:43.070
In fact, it made it harder.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:44.030
It made it tougher.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:44.960
Made it slower.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.770
You were confused
the entire time.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.050
And I call those Edsels.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.120
Do you know what an Edsel is?

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:56.470
The Edsel is the automotive
world's biggest flop.

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.180
So I'm going to tell
you a little bit

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:00.010
of a story about the Edsel.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:03.412
So in 1957, Ford is
hinting about the next car

00:01:03.412 --> 00:01:04.870
that they're going
to be producing,

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:06.440
the car of the future

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:09.940
it's gonna be. They've
spent 10 years developing

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:14.830
this thing, $250
million of 1950s money,

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:17.860
which is $2.5 billion today.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:20.620
And it's supposed to be this
fantastical thing that's

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.990
going to take us
all into the future,

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:25.510
and it's going to be full
of technologies that are

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:28.220
going to make our lives easier.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:33.380
In 1958, the Edsel arrives
with a lot of fanfare.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:34.940
They've got
advertisements all over

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:37.280
the world are all
over the states.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.150
They've got a TV show
about the Edsel, a TV show

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:43.230
about the Edsel, of all things.

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:45.510
And it's got all this
wonderful technology.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:48.480
Like in your steering wheel,
you can select your gear.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:49.950
You don't have to gear shift.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.250
It's just in your steering wheel,
it's right there for you.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.710
On the dashboard,
there's a button

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:57.740
that squirts oil on
your front suspension

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:59.040
to keep it from squeaking.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.730
Isn't that amazing?

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:04.050
But in 1959, they kill it.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:04.860
It's done.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.620
It's no longer
out there anymore.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:09.830
In 1960, the last Edsel
rolls off the line.

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:12.470
This is a screen capture
from a Times Magazine

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:16.140
with the wrong car in the
wrong place at the wrong time.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:17.882
But what happened
from 1957, where

00:02:17.882 --> 00:02:19.590
it's the car of the
future, it's supposed

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:23.660
to be doing everything
for us, to 1959

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:24.160
it's dead.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:24.800
It's gone.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.560
Stop asking about it.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:29.570
Well, there are a couple
of different problems

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:31.220
with the Edsel.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.450
First, Edsel was
designed by committee.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.710
There was no real why driving
the development of the Edsel

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:38.493
other than it's the
car of the future.

00:02:38.493 --> 00:02:39.660
It's got to have everything.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.930
It's going to be fantastic.

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:43.820
During this time,
also, Ford was going

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:45.600
through some restructuring,
some changing.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.330
And so a lot of
different executives

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:49.550
were in charge of this
project over the 10 years

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:51.470
it was being
developed, of course.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:54.470
So they all wanted to put
their thumbprint on the Edsel

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:57.290
to enable to push
their careers forward

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:00.330
without really paying respect
to what is this supposed to be?

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:02.840
What are we trying to
actually accomplish here?

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:05.120
And then, as I alluded
to earlier, 10 years

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:05.820
of development.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:09.050
So when it was first released,
they were all in on this.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:10.760
There were 18
different models that

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:13.160
were available for
people, not including

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.715
colors, internal trims,
all those other things.

00:03:15.715 --> 00:03:17.090
This was just all
the models that

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:20.030
were available for the Edsel.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:23.240
Then going on the idea that
there was no real why behind it,

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:26.120
there were no real
requirements or constraints

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:27.650
when they were designing it.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:31.220
So the car design, the horseshoe
that you saw at the front,

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:32.940
there was thought
to be really ugly.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.643
And in fact, people didn't
like the way that it looked.

00:03:35.643 --> 00:03:38.060
Now, I don't really think it
looks too bad, but, you know,

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:39.410
whatever.

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:42.110
And then there also had
a bunch of technology

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:44.430
that didn't really
solve any problems

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:47.360
for us. It was technological,
technological solutions that

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:48.980
were searching for a problem.

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:50.750
They were trying to
identify that. You know

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:53.125
those automatic gearshift
selector in the steering wheel?

00:03:53.125 --> 00:03:54.080
People hated it.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:55.670
You know what you're used to
in the center of your steering

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:56.170
wheel?

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:57.248
A horn.

00:03:57.248 --> 00:03:58.790
They'd hit the horn
and they'd actually

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:02.010
accidentally change gears.

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:04.590
In addition, there was a
lot of QA and QC issues

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:06.730
related to all those
technological solutions.

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:08.970
They weren't really
meant to be in production

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.850
that early, and that led
to issues where they were

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:15.060
the factory was
sending incomplete cars

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:17.425
to their dealerships with
just all the different pieces

00:04:17.425 --> 00:04:19.050
that they didn't
complete just sitting

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:23.450
in the boot waiting for the
dealerships to sort this out.

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:28.120
And so that's why, of many
other reasons, the Edsel failed.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.580
But how can we take
lessons from this

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:33.730
and keep our next project
from becoming the next Edsel?

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:36.430
So I'm going to go through a
little case study of a project

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:41.560
that we worked on where we
identified a slow process

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:43.000
that we wanted to fix.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.930
So inside of our
organization, we

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:49.210
have a programming team
that does all our data

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:51.280
analysis and data prep.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:53.020
They processes this data.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:54.760
They do all this
formatting and they

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:56.230
output to several
different files

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:58.120
and intending for
downstream users

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:02.350
to use this to generate reports
and presentations and reports

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:04.060
and whatnot.

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:06.190
These downstream
users take this,

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:10.378
the statisticians will generate
reports or presentations,

00:05:10.378 --> 00:05:11.920
and our medical
writers will generate

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:15.640
reports of differing levels
of programming backgrounds

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:16.840
and whatnot.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:19.090
The issue with this
is our programmers

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:21.610
are generating data
in formats that

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:24.127
our downstream users
couldn't use directly.

00:05:24.127 --> 00:05:26.710
That meant that there was a lot
of manual reproducing of data,

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:29.230
a lot of copy and
paste from the format

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:32.090
that we were producing
into their PowerPoint

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:34.090
and Word documents.

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:35.590
When people are
copying and pasting,

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:38.342
that leads to formatting
inconsistencies like, oh, I'll

00:05:38.342 --> 00:05:39.050
just delete that.

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:41.293
I don't, I don't like
the way that looks.

00:05:41.293 --> 00:05:42.710
I'll just put it
at the top there.

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:44.823
Or I want more spacing here.

00:05:44.823 --> 00:05:46.240
I want to merge
these columns, not

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:49.182
fundamentally changing the data,
but the way it's displayed.

00:05:49.182 --> 00:05:50.890
And because we're
doing all this copying,

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:53.170
pasting and
reformatting, we have

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:56.290
to do a lot of really intense QC
on data we've already generated

00:05:56.290 --> 00:06:00.190
and already QCed to make sure
that we're not showing anything

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:01.480
that's incorrect.

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:05.275
So there's all this effort
going into this process that

00:06:05.275 --> 00:06:07.150
is just we're not
producing the correct data,

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:09.025
we're not working, we're
not talking together

00:06:09.025 --> 00:06:12.570
to make sure that everything
works together nicely.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:14.080
But we saw this
as you know what?

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:15.570
That's not a bad thing.

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:18.090
That's an opportunity for us
to step in and help and try

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:21.210
to improve the process.
Because the data is being used

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:23.940
by the same downstream
user, the same data

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:25.820
are being used by
the downstream users,

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:27.940
we can use this as
a starting point,

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:30.060
and try to build processes
and build a package

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.390
to try to make
their lives easier.

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:35.660
Next, we found out and
learned about packages

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:37.820
that had come out like
the officeverse, which

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:41.330
is a series of packages by David
Gohel that interact with Office

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:44.870
products and help you write your
own XML to allow you to inject

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:47.810
and work with your
Word and PowerPoint

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:50.610
package or presentations
and documents and whatnot.

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:52.400
So officer. And there's
also flextable,

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:56.270
which is a package that
helps you make tables

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:59.150
into PowerPoints
and Word documents.

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:02.440
Kind of seems like it'll
be pretty useful for us.

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:05.740
So before we really went
and just went all full tilt

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:07.660
to develop anything, we
wanted to make sure we

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:09.050
had a full set of expectations

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:10.925
so we understood what
we were trying to build

00:07:10.925 --> 00:07:12.670
before we went to go build it.

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:14.410
So if we're designing
a new workflow,

00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:15.815
we want to create collaboration.

00:07:15.815 --> 00:07:17.440
That that was what
was causing problems

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:19.570
before between our
programmers, statisticians

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:20.800
and medical writers.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:23.060
We want it to work with
our existing systems.

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:25.720
We don't want to have to
try to create and roll up

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:26.925
a new system.

00:07:26.925 --> 00:07:27.800
What do we have that?

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:29.350
What can we reuse?

00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:31.370
Where can we extract
the data from?

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:34.350
How can we pull from
existing streams?

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:36.720
We're already using
PowerPoint and docx,

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:37.870
so let's output to that.

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:41.070
Let's not make our end
users relearn everything.

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:43.352
And then as we
generate these inputs,

00:07:43.352 --> 00:07:45.060
let's automatically
tag those because you

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:47.670
can use comments in
PowerPoint and docx,

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:49.455
tag those with
comments that, hey,

00:07:49.455 --> 00:07:50.830
you're going to
need to QC this.

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:53.620
This was generated custom.

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:56.680
And then ultimately this process
originally was very slow.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:08:01.900
We want to reduce the timeline
into less than a week.

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:03.580
Then as we went to
go develop the team,

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:05.200
now that we had
our expectations,

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:09.360
we had a team, a very small
team of experienced individuals

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:11.860
that were both understood the
process, what they were trying

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:15.010
to replace, as well as
understood what they were

00:08:15.010 --> 00:08:17.080
generating or how to program.

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:17.840
This is important.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:19.632
So we understood what
were the constraints,

00:08:19.632 --> 00:08:22.870
what could we feasibly
make or not make?

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:24.475
We had a single
point decision maker.

00:08:24.475 --> 00:08:26.600
They were ultimately
responsible for making a call

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:28.337
if there was a
disagreement. Then

00:08:28.337 --> 00:08:30.670
we worked with the experts,
the people that we're using,

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:33.100
our downstream users, as
well as upstream users

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:35.792
to define what are the core
things that we need to create,

00:08:35.792 --> 00:08:38.000
what are the features that
need to be on the roadmap

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.140
eventually? we maybe don't
need them immediately,

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:43.307
but ultimately they did not
have the final decision.

00:08:43.307 --> 00:08:45.640
They weren't the executives
at Ford that were telling us

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:46.240
what to do.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:49.580
They were saying, hey,
it'd be really nice if.

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:51.140
We tried to simplify things.

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:53.020
We didn't want to try
to recreate the wheel.

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:55.190
Like I said, we didn't
want to recreate systems.

00:08:55.190 --> 00:08:57.030
So let's use existing tools.

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:59.750
Let's use officeverse rather
than trying to make our home

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:02.390
cooked version of
officeverse to interact

00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:05.160
with the XML and PowerPoint
and docx.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:07.910
And let's use the tools
that already exist

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:11.480
and try to maybe improve
it as we need to.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:13.940
People were adding their own
standards in their own changes

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:14.790
to the outputs.

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:17.653
So let's revert back to
what are the standards?

00:09:17.653 --> 00:09:19.320
What exactly is it
that we have to make?

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:21.350
And then we can maybe
iterate from there.

00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:24.120
And then identify areas of
common rework by the staff.

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:26.570
What are the common
reformatting that is being done

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:28.380
and can we do this
programmatically?

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:30.180
Is it reasonable to do
it programmatically?

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:33.080
What are things that we need
to capitalize or lowercase

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:36.090
or change?

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:39.330
While we develop this too,
we wanted to watch our scope.

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:42.900
So we wanted to make sure that
the system could grow as we

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:44.700
needed, to change as we needed.

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:46.360
The world is a changing place.

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:48.270
Let's try to keep that in mind.

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:49.740
The feature requests
that we got,

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:52.710
we would compare them against
the feasibility of doing that.

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:54.550
Once again, can we do
this programmatically?

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:56.258
Should we be doing
this programmatically?

00:09:56.258 --> 00:09:57.900
Or should we push back and go,

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:00.610
maybe this isn't the time.

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:02.240
We also have limited resources.

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:04.520
We have a small team of
people working on this.

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:08.140
So that made us keep in mind
that everything that we did

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:09.917
was in strict
adherence to the goals.

00:10:09.917 --> 00:10:11.500
What are we actually
trying to achieve

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:13.185
and try to push away things?

00:10:13.185 --> 00:10:14.810
This might sound a
little bit familiar.

00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:16.020
This is kind of agile.

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:17.770
I don't know if you've
ever heard of that.

00:10:17.770 --> 00:10:18.687
That's pretty popular.

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:24.450
So I'm happy to report that
this actually this project did

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:25.630
turn out pretty well.

00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:27.750
We've reduced the reporting
time significantly,

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:30.450
things we've been able
to generate reports

00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:32.160
in under a week.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:35.340
There's no programming skills
required by our non programming

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:35.840
staff.

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:38.610
They are not required to
write any programming.

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:43.650
We're able to generate their
reports using the programmers

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:46.270
and then just running it through
this tool that we created.

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:48.750
And as everyone
asks in pharma, it

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:54.510
is validated per our
internal process.

00:10:54.510 --> 00:10:56.460
So the reason this
project succeeded,

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:59.217
I believe it named a
couple of those as well.

00:10:59.217 --> 00:11:00.550
We established our requirements.

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:01.620
We knew what our
outputs were, we

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:03.078
knew what we were
trying to create.

00:11:03.078 --> 00:11:05.160
And we didn't deviate from that.

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:07.210
We knew what our
measurement of success was.

00:11:07.210 --> 00:11:10.920
We knew if we achieved
this goal, we succeeded.

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:12.840
And anything that
we did was only

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:17.753
in trying to achieve that goal.
We were relentless. When people

00:11:17.753 --> 00:11:19.670
asked us to do new
features, add new features,

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:21.295
we'd go, OK, do you
need this now or is

00:11:21.295 --> 00:11:23.140
this something can happen later?

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:25.420
We kept perspective of the
values that we were adding

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:28.605
and tried, once again,
it's all about not

00:11:28.605 --> 00:11:29.980
doing things we
don't need to do.

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:33.370
Don't add unnecessary
things unless you need to.

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:35.590
And then also single
point decision making.

00:11:35.590 --> 00:11:40.010
Ultimately, one person
had to make a call.

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:43.660
OK I'm going to close
with a final story

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:46.540
from the same company, Ford.

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:48.940
And we're going to make
a GT, not an Edsel.

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:59.030
So 1966, Ford wins the
world renowned hardest race

00:11:59.030 --> 00:12:00.660
in the racing world,

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:04.160
the 24 Hours of Le Mans, which
is a race where people drive

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:05.930
for 24 hours and the
winner is determined

00:12:05.930 --> 00:12:08.640
not by how many laps they
do, but how far they drive.

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:11.760
This is a true endurance test
of the quality of your car.

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:12.690
How good is it made?

00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:15.070
How well did you design this?

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:17.140
The impressive thing
about this, Ford,

00:12:17.140 --> 00:12:21.370
this is the second year Ford
had ever competed in Le Mans.

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:25.300
They'd never competed in
an competed in it before.

00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:27.160
The prior winner
had been Ferrari,

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:29.080
who'd won for the
last six years and has

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:31.190
a long history in racing.

00:12:31.190 --> 00:12:34.100
So how did Ford go
from a company that

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:35.450
made a thing like the Edsel

00:12:35.450 --> 00:12:40.540
to the GT and won
the whole darn thing

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:43.820
in their second
year of competing?

00:12:43.820 --> 00:12:46.130
Well, they had a
small, dedicated team.

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:48.840
They set out to beat Ferrari.

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:52.460
That was their goal in mind,
not just win for the 24 hours

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:55.250
of Le Mans, but
let's beat Ferrari.

00:12:55.250 --> 00:12:59.240
They get they got together a
small team of Carroll Shelby

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:02.240
and Ken Miles, who had
a history of success

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:03.850
doing this sort of thing.

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:06.660
Carroll Shelby was an old
racer, so he used to race.

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:08.420
He actually won Le Mans

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:11.270
I think a decade or two before
and started his own car company

00:13:11.270 --> 00:13:12.580
called Shelby American.

00:13:12.580 --> 00:13:15.490
You might know him
from the Shelby Cobra.

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:17.590
As well as Ken
Miles, who had been

00:13:17.590 --> 00:13:22.390
a successful driver in
Britain, racing various cars

00:13:22.390 --> 00:13:24.380
of various degrees.

00:13:24.380 --> 00:13:27.590
And then everything they
did for the next two years

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:29.260
was focused on results.

00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:31.650
What could they do to
make the GT faster?

00:13:31.650 --> 00:13:34.190
How could they win Le Mans?

00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:37.380
They worked
through aerodynamics.

00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:39.150
They had an initial concept.

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:40.850
They did.

00:13:40.850 --> 00:13:42.058
They constantly drove around.

00:13:42.058 --> 00:13:44.433
They had the drivers that
were going to be in the race

00:13:44.433 --> 00:13:46.760
drive the car around, give
feedback to the engineers.

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:49.700
Even this is a picture of
the engineers and drivers

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:54.500
with little pieces of string
that they taped onto the GT

00:13:54.500 --> 00:13:56.595
to see how aerodynamics
worked and if there

00:13:56.595 --> 00:13:58.220
were any areas of low
pressure that they

00:13:58.220 --> 00:13:59.990
would need to address.

00:13:59.990 --> 00:14:02.450
And they trusted the
drivers experience.

00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:04.700
In addition to that, they
pushed technologies forward.

00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:08.270
They took the Ford's proven
racing engine from NASCAR

00:14:08.270 --> 00:14:09.120
and improved it.

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:11.030
They made it faster,
they made it stronger.

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:13.830
They took existing technologies
and made it better.

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:15.290
And they also innovated.

00:14:15.290 --> 00:14:18.470
They took the quick change or
they invented the quick change

00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:20.070
brake pads and rotors.

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:21.450
The GT was a very heavy car.

00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:23.210
They break a lot
in Le Mans or they

00:14:23.210 --> 00:14:24.930
have to press the brake pad.

00:14:24.930 --> 00:14:27.292
So they needed a
quick way to redo this.

00:14:27.292 --> 00:14:29.000
So they created new
technologies in order

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:31.030
to solve the problems
at hand, and that was

00:14:31.030 --> 00:14:32.280
all they were ever focused on.

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:33.540
How can we do this faster?

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:36.840
How can we do this better?

00:14:36.840 --> 00:14:38.480
So I'm going to end
with this. Projects

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:40.080
and packages should be
designed with purpose

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:41.880
and be relentless in
pursuing their goals.

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:45.120
That's how you make a
good package and process.

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:46.910
Thank you.

